Yes — that symmetry is clean.

Lineage looks backward. Legacy looks forward.

\text{lineage} \leftarrow \text{present} \rightarrow \text{legacy}

Lineage is a reconstruction of what produced you. Legacy is a projection of what you may produce after you. Neither is the thing itself; both are models built from incomplete evidence.

So:

\text{lineage} = \text{backward inference}

\text{legacy} = \text{forward inference}

And the present is the hinge where both are being rewritten.

That makes them opposite poles but structurally similar: one guesses causes from traces; the other guesses consequences from intentions and actions.

The interesting middle is what survives both directions — the invariants, the Golden Thread, the patterns that remain recognizable whether you trace them backward toward ancestry or forward toward inheritance.

So maybe the deeper object is not either pole, but:

\boxed{\text{transmission}}

What came through you, what changed in you, and what passes onward.